    "case_id": "67",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 80s presented to the emergency department with altered mental status, weakness, slurred speech, emesis, and headache. Emergency medical services found her pulse rate in the 20s. Her initial vital signs in the emergency department were blood pressure of 100/73 mm Hg, heart rate of 49 beats/min, respiratory rate of 27 breaths/min, and oxygen saturation of 95% on a nonrebreather mask.\n\nPatient required intubation for airway protection.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    1. Computed tomography of the head: No acute pathology    2. Chest radiography: Showed pulmonary consolidation    3. Electrocardiogram (ECG):       Image Title: Electrocardiogram During Transcutaneous Pacing       Image Description: ECG showing intrinsic junctional rhythm at rate of about 60 beats/min, and TCP delivering stimuli at about 75 beats/min. Narrow QRS complexes without visible preceding P waves at rate of about 60 beats/min.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Sepsis with encephalopathy\nB. Septic shock with anuric kidney failure\nC. Sepsis secondary to Pneumonia\nD. Pneumonia leading to sepsis and encephalopathy\n",
